<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Zacazgo of Hunza</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1537 CE (approx.) · capital Hunza</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern Muisca realm of the zaque, ruled from Hunza, the site of modern Tunja, and long the rival of the zipa of Bacatá.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hunza-state">Hunza</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a>, became the seat of the zaque, ruler of the northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/muisca-confederation">Muisca</a> territories, in the centuries before the Spanish conquest. Muisca tradition named Hunzahúa as its first ruler and claimed the zaques descended from him.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The zaque ruled through lesser chiefs and received tribute in gold, cloth and goods. Succession passed from a ruler to his sister's son. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hunza-state">Hunza</a> was a centre of trade in salt, emeralds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and gold. The zaques fought repeated wars with the zipas of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zipazgo-of-bacata">Zipazgo of Bacatá</a>, including a battle in which the zipa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saguamanchica">Saguamanchica</a> and the zaque <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michua">Michuá</a> were both killed around 1490.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1537 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hunza-state">Hunza</a> and captured the aged zaque <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quemuenchatocha">Quemuenchatocha</a>. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquiminzaque">Aquiminzaque</a> was executed in 1540. The Spanish founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a> on the site in 1539, and it became a major town of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-kingdom-of-granada">New Kingdom of Granada</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1300 CE – 1537 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.57%;width:4.72%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1537 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hunza</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Michuá, Quemuenchatocha, Aquiminzaque</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Muisca religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Muisca (Chibcha)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hunza, Zacazgo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1418&amp;focus=zacazgo-of-hunza" aria-label="Zacazgo of Hunza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2647 1976 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zacazgo of Hunza lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2967 2232L2942 2257M2967 2232L4910 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2942 2257h0M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2967 2232h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2967 2232h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hunza. <a href="/atlas?year=1418&amp;focus=zacazgo-of-hunza">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

